_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 66
---
order: 66
---
short_label: X.66
---
statement: A straight line commensurable with a binomial straight line is itself also binomial and the same in order.
---
proof:

Let *AB* be binomial, and let *CD* be commensurable in length with *AB*.

I say that *CD* is binomial and the same in order with *AB*.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="250" height="95" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="X.66 diagram" src="propX66.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "X.66",
        elements: [
            "A0;point;free;-1000,30;0;0",
            "A3;point;free;1000,30;0;0",
            "C0;point;free;-1000,65;0;0",
            "C3;point;free;1000,65;0;0",
            "A;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,60,0",
            "B;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,180,0",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "C;point;lineSlider;C0,C3,30,0",
            "D;point;lineSlider;C0,C3,210,0",
            "CD;line;connect;C,D",
            "E;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,130,0",
            "F;point;proportion;A,B,C,D,A,E,C,C3"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

Since *AB* is binomial, divide it into its terms at *E*, and let *AE* be the greater term, therefore *AE* and *EB* are rational straight lines commensurable in square only. [!just X.36]

Let it be contrived that *AB* is to *CD* as *AE* is to *CF*. Then the remainder *EB* is to the remainder *FD* as *AB* is to *CD*. [!just VI.12, V.19]

But *AB* is commensurable in length with *CD*, therefore *AE* is also commensurable with *CF*, and *EB* with *FD*. [!just X.11]

And *AE* and *EB* are rational, therefore *CF* and *FD* are also rational.

And *AE* is to *CF* as *EB* is to *FD*. Therefore, alternately, *AE* is to *EB* as *CF* is to *FD*. [!just V.11, V.16]

But *AE* and *EB* are commensurable in square only, therefore *CF* and *FD* are also commensurable in square only. [!just X.11]

And they are rational, therefore *CD* is binomial. [!just X.36]

I say next that it is the same in order with *AB*.

For the square on *AE* is greater than the square on *EB* either by the square on a straight line commensurable with *AE* or by the square on a straight line incommensurable with it.

If then the square on *AE* is greater than the square on *EB* by the square on a straight line commensurable with *AE*, then the square on *CF* is also greater than the square on *FD* by the square on a straight line commensurable with *CF*. [!just X.14]

And, if *AE* is commensurable with the rational straight line set out, then *CF* is also commensurable with it, and for this reason each of the straight lines *AB* and *CD* is a first binomial, that is, the same in order. [!just X.12; X.Def.II.1]

But, if *EB* is commensurable with the rational straight line set out, then *FD* is also commensurable with it, and for this reason again *CD* is the same in order with *AB*, for each of them is a second binomial. [!just X.12; X.Def.II.2]

But, if neither of the straight lines *AE* nor *EB* is commensurable with the rational straight line set out, then neither of the straight lines *CF* nor *FD* is commensurable with it, and each of the straight lines *AB* and *CD* is a third binomial. [!just X.13; X.Def.II.3]

But, if the square on *AE* is greater than the square on *EB* by the square on a straight line incommensurable with *AE*, then the square on *CF* is also greater than the square on *FD* by the square on a straight line incommensurable with *CF*. [!just X.14]

And, if *AE* is commensurable with the rational straight line set out, then *CF* is also commensurable with it, and each of the straight lines *AB* and *CD* is a fourth binomial. [!just X.Def.II.4]

But, if *EB* is so commensurable, then *FD* is also, and each of the straight lines *AB* and *CD* is a fifth binomial. [!just X.Def.II.5]

But, if neither of the straight lines *AE* or *EB* is so commensurable, then neither of the straight lines *CF* or *FD* is commensurable with the rational straight line set out, and each of the straight lines *AB* and *CD* is a sixth binomial. [!just X.Def.II.6]

Hence a straight line commensurable in length with a binomial straight line is binomial and the same in order.

Therefore, *a straight line commensurable in length with a binomial straight line is itself also binomial and the same in order.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
---
body:

This proposition is not used in the rest of the *Elements.*

